Move MusicOggStream's 44 KiB PCM staging buffer off the stack.

streamIntoBuffer() declared `char pcm[m_buffer_size]` with m_buffer_size =
11025*4 = 44100, i.e. a 44 KiB stack frame. It runs on the SFX thread, which
SFXManager creates through std::thread and therefore with a NULL pthread
attr, so it gets libphoenix's default stack: 4096 bytes
(ALIGN(PTHREAD_STACK_MIN, PAGE_SIZE)) with guardsize 0, hence no guard page.

ov_read then filled 44100 bytes upward starting ~40 KiB below the thread's
own stack region, silently overwriting whatever was mapped there.

Proven by byte-exact match, not inferred: a kernel dump of the faulting
stack was located verbatim in decoded menutheme.ogg PCM, and two separate
crashes' corrupted return addresses sat exactly 44100 bytes apart in that
stream -- the same intra-chunk offset in consecutive streamIntoBuffer()
calls, which is why the faulting address was reproducible across runs.

Depending on what the excursion landed on this surfaced either as a jump to
a garbage address (a neighbouring thread's saved LR, a different victim
thread each run) or as a fault inside malloc (a smashed chunk header) -- two
symptoms of one overflow.

